  {
   "id": "b2",
   "function": "problem",
   "scene_type": "zlx_cutaway",
   "spoken": "In the compression stroke the piston moves up. Both valves are closed, so the air has less and less room. Largest volume divided by smallest volume gives the compression ratio. The work done by the piston ends up as heat in the air. The piston does the squeezing, and the air has nowhere to go.",
   "data": {
    "title": "Compression",
    "fuel": "diesel",
    "labels": [
     "chamber"
    ],
    "labels_at": [
     "w:room"
    ],
    "notes": [
     {
      "text": "Valves closed",
      "at": "w:valves",
      "side": "l",
      "y": 760,
      "to": [
       452,
       432
      ]
     }
    ],
    "theta": [
     [
      0,
      200
     ],
     [
      1,
      355
     ]
    ],
    "fade_in": false,
    "fade_out": false,
    "source": "NASA Glenn, compression stroke · retrieved 2026-11-01"
   },
   "overlay": {},
   "source_ids": [
    "S1"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "b3",
   "function": "explanation",
   "scene_type": "zlx_readout",
   "spoken": "NASA describes it with two formulas. Pressure rises with the compression ratio to the power of gamma, temperature with gamma minus one. For air, gamma is about one point four.",
   "data": {
    "title": "Two formulas",
    "figure": "cylinder",
    "lines_y": 1000,
    "bore_label": "Air",
    "stroke_label": "Volume",
    "stroke_at": "w:compression",
    "fill_at": 0.05,
    "lines": [
     {
      "text": "p₂ / p₁ = ε ^ γ",
      "hl": "γ",
      "at": "w:Pressure"
     },
     {
      "text": "T₂ / T₁ = ε ^ (γ − 1)",
      "hl": "γ − 1",
      "at": "w:temperature"
     },
     {
      "text": "γ ≈ 1.4 for air",
      "hl": "1.4",
      "at": "w:air"
     }
    ],
    "theta": [
     [
      0,
      180
     ],
     [
      1,
      540
     ]
    ],
    "fade_in": false,
    "fade_out": false,
    "source": "NASA Glenn, compression stroke · retrieved 2026-11-01"
   },
   "overlay": {},
   "source_ids": [
    "S1"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "b4",
   "function": "demonstration",
   "scene_type": "zlx_readout",
   "spoken": "An example, calculated ideally: a compression ratio of sixteen to one and intake air at twenty degrees. The temperature rises by about a factor of three, to around six hundred degrees Celsius. That is the ideal value, without heat loss to the walls. Gamma minus one is zero point four, and sixteen to the power of zero point four is about three.",
   "data": {
    "title": "Example 16 : 1",
    "figure": "bars",
    "bar_max": 640,
    "lines_y": 1000,
    "bars": [
     {
      "label": "Intake air",
      "value": 20,
      "text": "20 °C",
      "at": "w:intake"
     },
     {
      "label": "after compression (ideal)",
      "value": 615,
      "text": "≈ 615 °C",
      "at": "w:Celsius"
     }
    ],
    "lines": [
     {
      "text": "16 ^ 0.4 ≈ 3.0",
      "hl": "3.0",
      "at": "w:factor"
     },
     {
      "text": "293 K × 3.0 ≈ 890 K",
      "hl": "890 K",
      "at": "w:around"
     }
    ],
    "theta": [
     [
      0,
      0
     ],
     [
      1,
      360
     ]
    ],
    "fade_in": false,
    "fade_out": false,
    "source": "NASA Glenn, compression stroke · retrieved 2026-11-01"
   },
   "overlay": {},
   "source_ids": [
    "S1"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "b5",
   "function": "insight",
   "scene_type": "zlx_cutaway",
   "spoken": "That heat is exactly what a diesel uses. It injects the fuel into the hot air, and the fuel ignites on its own. It needs no spark plug for that. The US Department of Energy calls this ignition by the heat of compression. No spark, no plug, just heat.",
   "data": {
    "title": "Self-ignition",
    "fuel": "diesel",
    "notes": [
